<com:TContent ID="body" >
<h1>Configuration Overview</h1>
<p class="block-content">
PRADO uses configurations to glue together components into pages and applications. There are <a href="?page=Configurations.AppConfig">application configurations</a>, <a href="?page=Configurations.PageConfig">page configurations</a>, and <a href="?page=Configurations.Templates1">templates</a>.
</p>
<p class="block-content">
All three are optional. An application with no configuration at all runs on the default
modules and the page service, and a page with no template renders whatever its class
writes.
</p>

<h2>The three kinds</h2>

<table class="parameters">
<tr><th>Kind</th><th>Lives in</th><th>Sets</th></tr>
<tr>
	<td><a href="?page=Configurations.AppConfig">Application configuration</a></td>
	<td>One file at the application base path, <tt>application.xml</tt> or <tt>application.php</tt>.</td>
	<td>Path aliases, modules, services, parameters, and the properties of the application itself.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
	<td><a href="?page=Configurations.PageConfig">Page configuration</a></td>
	<td><tt>config.xml</tt> or <tt>config.php</tt>, in any page directory.</td>
	<td>The same, narrowed to that directory, plus authorization rules and initial page property values.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
	<td><a href="?page=Configurations.Templates1">Template</a></td>
	<td>A <tt>.page</tt> or <tt>.tpl</tt> file beside the class that uses it.</td>
	<td>The controls on a page or inside a control, and their initial property values.</td>
</tr>
</table>

<h2>How they combine</h2>

<p class="block-content">
The application configuration is read once, when the application starts. Page
configurations are read afterwards, and only for the request at hand: the page service
walks from the base path down to the directory holding the requested page, reading a
configuration file in each directory it passes. Each one appends to or overrides what came
before, so a setting made near the root applies to everything below it and a setting made
in a leaf directory applies to that directory alone.
</p>

<p class="block-content">
An application chooses XML or PHP for both kinds at once. The choice is made in the entry
script, where the application is constructed, and it decides which file names the
framework looks for.
</p>

<h2>Other configuration in this chapter</h2>

<ul id="u1" class="block-content">
  <li><a href="?page=Configurations.UrlMapping">URL mapping</a> turns request paths into service and page requests.</li>
  <li><a href="?page=Configurations.BehaviorsConfig">Behaviors configuration</a> attaches <a href="?page=Fundamentals.Behaviors">behaviors</a> to classes and objects without changing their code.</li>
  <li><a href="?page=Configurations.Parameters">Database parameters</a> keep application parameters in a database table rather than in a file.</li>
</ul>

</com:TContent>
